---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Brief = `The arcade cabinet keeps a list of scores. I want the top
score, who got it, and how far off the average that top score
is. The names and the scores are in two separate lists that
line up.`;

const SampleOutput = `High Scores
-----------
Avery   8400
Blake   9150
Casey   7300
Dakota  9150

Top score: 9150 (Blake)
Average: 8500.0
Top is 650 above average.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-1/cc-set-4" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back to Set 4
        </a>

        <h1>Code Challenge: High Score Table</h1>

        <h4>
          Difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="brief">
          <h2>The Brief</h2>

          <Code
            code={Brief}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="What they asked for"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b>Write your requirements list before you write any code.</b> Input,
            calculate, output, and anything you had to decide for yourself. See{" "}
            <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-1/cc-set-4">Set 4</a> for what that looks like.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="notes">
          <h2>Notes</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>Two arrays, kept in step by index. That is the parallel-array idea from Activity 1.25.</li>
            <li>Finding the highest score is a running-max loop. Finding <i>who</i> got it means also tracking the position where you found it.</li>
            <li>That position is the whole challenge. The name lives at the same index in the other array.</li>
            <li><b>Test what happens if the two arrays are different lengths.</b> Activity 1.25 warned about this for a reason.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="sample-output">
          <h2>Sample Output</h2>

          <Code
            code={SampleOutput}
            frame="terminal"
            title="Sample Output"
            lang="txt"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-3">Blake and Dakota are tied deliberately. Decide which one your program reports and say why in your requirements list.</p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Your requirements list and your program file, named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">cc-high-score-table</b>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Challenge
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
